# Every text file is LF, in the repository and in the working tree.
#
# Without this, core.autocrlf=true on Windows checks files out as CRLF while the
# repository stores LF, so git reports a pending change on files nobody edited and
# warns on every add. The working-tree half matters beyond tidiness: these sources
# are copied into Linux container images, and CRLF reaching a shell script or an
# entrypoint there is a real failure rather than a cosmetic one.
#
# `text=auto` still lets git detect binary content and leave it alone. Nothing
# tracked here is binary today, so no per-type overrides are needed yet.
* text=auto eol=lf
